{
  "dom_id": "step-q65135",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$ and $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=f\\,∇\\,g+g\\,∇\\,f\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the first order partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\text{ }j=2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =({e}^{x+y})(2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j)+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})({e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+2y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+2y+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$",
